Image generation task:
- Produce one polished educational flashcard visual for the already-planned card. The image should
  support recall of the back answer, not replace the written card.
- Treat the card-specific brief, question, answer, layout, and reference policy as the source of
  truth. If they conflict with a reference image, follow the brief and answer.
- Use sparse phone-readable labels only when they improve recall. Prefer 1-3 large labels/callouts,
  arrows, contrast, grouping, or visual consequence over dense text.
- Keep humor and memorability tied to the exact tested fact. The joke, story beat, or exaggeration
  must explain the relationship being studied.
- Do not optimize, abbreviate, count, or rename source facts unless the scenario explicitly asks.
- Do not invent numeric values, dial readings, units, counts, frequencies, altitudes, pressures, or
  legal limits. If an instrument/control concept needs a label and the source did not give exact
  values, use generic labels such as "setting", "reads field elevation", "local pressure", arrows,
  or blank/abstract gauge marks instead of made-up numbers. Never show impossible aviation units or
  values.
- Never invent aviation/legal facts, institutional logos, seals, registrations, or official-looking
  markings.
